So `normalizeStringEntries` just trims the strings and filters empty ones - it doesn't strip the "whatsapp:" prefix.

Now I need to understand how the "whatsapp:" prefix should be stripped. Looking at the `resolveOwnerAllowFromList` function (lines 117-142), I can see it handles prefixes like "whatsapp:+123":

```javascript
const separatorIndex = trimmed.indexOf(":");
if (separatorIndex > 0) {
  const prefix = trimmed.slice(0, separatorIndex);
  const channel = normalizeAnyChannelId(prefix);
  if (channel) {
    if (params.providerId && channel !== params.providerId) {
      continue;
    }
    const remainder = trimmed.slice(separatorIndex + 1).trim();
    if (remainder) {
      filtered.push(remainder);
    }
    continue;
  }
}
filtered.push(trimmed);
```

So when the entry has a prefix like "whatsapp:", it extracts the remainder after the colon. But `normalizeAllowFromEntry` doesn't do this - it just calls `formatAllowFromList` which calls `normalizeStringEntries`.

Let me check if there's a plugin-specific `formatAllowFrom` for WhatsApp that might handle this.

Actually wait, I need to look at the issue more carefully. Let me check what changed recently. The issue says "kimi2.5 regression" but it's actually about heartbeat/exec-event command authorization. Let me look at the git log to see what changed recently in command-auth.ts.

